---
title: "Vector Database"
category: "Storage & Data Platforms"
difficulty: "Hard"
tags: ["vector-search", "ann", "hnsw", "filters", "lsm", "rocksdb", "s3"]
---

## Overview

A vector database is a specialized storage engine for high-dimensional embeddings that supports fast approximate nearest neighbor (ANN) search plus attribute filtering (tenant, time, tags, ACLs). The elegant core is treating the ANN index like an LSM-tree: write optimized, immutable “segments” that each contain their own ANN structure, then merge segments in the background. Queries search multiple segments and merge top‑k results.

This design avoids the trap of trying to keep a single global ANN graph perfectly up-to-date under continuous writes. Instead, we make the hot path boring: append a WAL, build small immutable segments, and periodically compact. The hard parts become well-scoped: filtered ANN retrieval and segment compaction without latency cliffs.

## What Makes This Hard

Naive systems die on two issues:

1) **Updates + deletes + recall**: HNSW/IVF indexes are great for static corpora, but continuous upserts and deletes degrade graph quality, explode memory, or force frequent rebuilds. Teams either accept silent recall loss or end up rebuilding giant indexes too often.



